WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.831
A student wants a scheduling app to read
free-and-busy information from a university

2
00:00:05.831 --> 00:00:06.276
calendar.

3
00:00:06.276 --> 00:00:11.623
The app should not receive the student's
password, and it should not gain unlimited

4
00:00:11.623 --> 00:00:12.005
access.

5
00:00:12.005 --> 00:00:14.544
OAuth solves this delegation problem.

6
00:00:14.544 --> 00:00:16.953
The student is the resource owner.

7
00:00:16.953 --> 00:00:19.205
The scheduling app is the client.

8
00:00:19.205 --> 00:00:25.616
The university identity service acts as
authorization server, and the calendar API is

9
00:00:25.616 --> 00:00:27.096
the resource server.

10
00:00:27.096 --> 00:00:32.902
Keeping these roles separate is the foundation
for reasoning about who authenticates, who

11
00:00:32.902 --> 00:00:37.369
grants access, who receives tokens, and who
stores protected data.

12
00:00:37.514 --> 00:00:42.594
Before redirecting the browser, the client
creates two independent values.

13
00:00:42.594 --> 00:00:47.677
State binds the returning authorization
response to the browser session and helps

14
00:00:47.677 --> 00:00:48.948
detect request forgery.

15
00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:54.625
The PKCE code verifier is a high-entropy
secret held temporarily by the client.

16
00:00:54.625 --> 00:01:00.409
The client hashes it to form a code challenge
that can safely travel in the authorization

17
00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:00.771
request.

18
00:01:00.771 --> 00:01:03.479
These mechanisms solve different problems.

19
00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:09.471
State protects the transaction's browser
context; PKCE later proves that the same

20
00:01:09.471 --> 00:01:13.466
client instance redeeming the code initiated
the request.

21
00:01:13.586 --> 00:01:19.971
The browser carries an authorization request
to the authorization server with client

22
00:01:19.971 --> 00:01:24.759
identifier, redirect URI, requested scope,
state, and PKCE challenge.

23
00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:30.514
The student authenticates directly with the
university service and reviews the requested

24
00:01:30.514 --> 00:01:33.392
permission, such as reading calendar
availability.

25
00:01:33.392 --> 00:01:36.569
The password never passes through the
scheduling app.

26
00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:41.977
If approved, the authorization server
redirects the browser back with a short-lived

27
00:01:41.977 --> 00:01:44.681
authorization code and the original state.

28
00:01:44.681 --> 00:01:50.786
Because this front channel is visible to the
browser and redirects, the code is not yet an

29
00:01:50.786 --> 00:01:51.504
access token.

30
00:01:51.626 --> 00:01:56.120
The client first verifies that returned state
matches the stored value.

31
00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:02.391
Its backend then sends the authorization code,
redirect URI, and original code verifier

32
00:02:02.391 --> 00:02:06.732
directly to the token endpoint over a
protected channel.

33
00:02:06.732 --> 00:02:12.279
The authorization server hashes the verifier
and compares it with the earlier challenge.

34
00:02:12.279 --> 00:02:17.031
An attacker who intercepted only the code
cannot complete this proof.

35
00:02:17.031 --> 00:02:24.074
If checks pass, the server returns a scoped
access token and, when policy permits, a

36
00:02:24.074 --> 00:02:25.013
refresh token.

37
00:02:25.013 --> 00:02:30.690
Tokens remain server-side and should be
encrypted at rest, narrowly scoped, and

38
00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.109
handled as credentials.

39
00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:37.918
The scheduling backend presents the access
token to the calendar API, which validates the

40
00:02:37.918 --> 00:02:41.572
token and scope before returning only the
authorized data.

41
00:02:41.572 --> 00:02:49.416
A security review now walks every boundary:
exact redirect-URI matching, state validation,

42
00:02:49.416 --> 00:02:57.259
PKCE verification, TLS, minimal scopes, token
lifetime, secure storage, revocation, and log

43
00:02:57.259 --> 00:02:57.913
redaction.

44
00:02:57.913 --> 00:03:01.767
OAuth does not make an application secure
automatically.

45
00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:07.794
It supplies a protocol for delegated access;
the implementation must preserve the

46
00:03:07.794 --> 00:03:08.798
protocol's assumptions.

47
00:03:08.798 --> 00:03:15.361
The most useful test is to ask what an
attacker gains from a stolen code, browser

48
00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:18.642
history, log entry, access token, or refresh
token.
